 #define GEO_MAX_BATCHES 64
 
+// What picks a cluster's mip level as the PS2 did (TASKS 82), one for
+// each of the mesh's boxes (geocull.h), in its own space.
+//
+// The GS takes the level from the depth: log2(1/Q) + K, with each strip's
+// K made once by eSolid::CalcAutoK (0x0010bfe8) from its entry --
+// 16 log2(sqrt(MaterialDensity * Width * Height / div)), where div makes a
+// strip that lies flat up to a level coarser. VU1 puts K's low byte in
+// TEX1 for each strip. So the level is what a strip facing the camera
+// would need, however the strip is turned. The PICA takes it from how far
+// the texels move along the screen, the most they move along x or y: a
+// wall seen side-on is a run of texels squeezed into a few pixels across,
+// and it takes a level as much as four coarser than the PS2 did, which
+// the user saw as blocks that flickered before the wall by track 11's
+// tunnel. georender_draw sets the LOD register's bias to the difference
+// for each run of clusters: the level the GS would take, less the one the
+// PICA will, from the camera's place against the cluster's plane and the
+// way its ST run across it.
+//
+// `gs` and `gt` are S's and T's change along a unit of the model's space,
+// the cluster's triangles' averaged by area; `n` is their plane's normal,
+// or 0 when they make no plane (less than 60% of the area within 25
+// degrees of it), and then the draw counts them as facing the camera.
+// `d` is log2(MaterialDensity / div), averaged by area over the strips
+// with a density; GEO_LOD_KZERO when most have none, which CalcAutoK makes
+// K 0.
+typedef struct {
+    float gs[3];
+    float gt[3];
+    float n[3];
+    float d;
+} GeoLod;
+
+#define GEO_LOD_KZERO -1000.0f
